  53.5s | {"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011Cej9YC6bt5QfhXsA4DTfa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013LFadbTWH7ZvgQtU5HNwZY","name":"mcp__hercules_ask__ask_user","input":{"questions":[{"question":"Question 3 — what \"keep both\" should actually mean to the user.\n\nWhen the same note was edited on two devices while one was offline, which of these is the behavior you want?\n\n- **Fork the note**: the note splits into two visible notes (\"Grocery list\" and \"Grocery list (conflicted copy from iPhone, Mar 3)\"), user reconciles by hand. Simple, honest, never loses a byte — but clutters the list.\n- **Merge automatically, keep both edits**: character-level merge (CRDT) so both devices' typing lands in one note and nobody sees a conflict at all. Best UX, most machinery, and can produce interleaved nonsense if both edited the same sentence.\n- **Auto-merge when the edits don't overlap, fork when they do**: middle ground, more moving parts.\n- **Keep one, but preserve the loser as version history**: the list stays clean, the other version is recoverable from a \"history\" screen rather than sitting in the note list.\n\nAlso: does concurrent editing of the *same* note on two devices actually happen for your users (shared accounts, phone + tablet), or is \"conflict\" mostly the rare case of one person's second device having stale state?","options":["Fork into a visibly conflicted copy","Auto-merge everything with a CRDT","Auto-merge non-overlapping, fork on true overlap","Keep one, loser goes to version history"]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":739,"cache_read_input_tokens":20936,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":739},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"